Date: 2026-04-24
Descriptor: sampling_scale_benchmark (ADR 0050)
Purpose:
    Push the scale characterization from ADR 0041 to 1000 edges
    using the sampling-mode path introduced in ADR 0043. Compare
    sampling vs. exhaustive at overlapping sizes so the trade-off
    (completeness vs. tractability) is visible in concrete
    numbers.

Mechanism version:
    ADRs 0001–0049. `instance_sampling = Some(cfg)` in
    `DriveConfig` routes `autonomous_pass` through
    `sample_instances_of` (ADR 0024 / 0043). `sample_count = 500`
    per candidate.

====================================================================
RESULTS
====================================================================

| edges | ids | mode       | steps | final score | wall time    |
|------:|----:|-----------|------:|------------:|-------------:|
|   100 |  48 | sampling   |   3   |   1 159.80  |   1.72 s     |
|   100 |  48 | exhaustive |   2   |   3 948.40  |   2.39 s     |
|   200 |  50 | sampling   |   3   |   1 109.00  |   2.46 s     |
|   200 |  50 | exhaustive |   2   |  20 237.70  |  38.22 s     |
|   500 | 100 | sampling   |   3   |   1 784.40  |  13.97 s     |
|  1000 | 100 | sampling   |   3   |   1 059.30  |  16.53 s     |

Exhaustive mode not run at 500 / 1000 edges — would take minutes
to hours based on ADR 0041's growth curve.

====================================================================
OBSERVATIONS
====================================================================

### Sampling scales where exhaustive cannot

At 1000 edges the sampling-mode drive completes in 16.5 s. ADR
0041 projected ≥ 17 min for this size under exhaustive mode
(based on O(edges × degree^(k-1)) growth). Sampling pushes the
interactive envelope from ~50 edges to ~1000 edges — a 20×
improvement.

### Sampling under-reports instance counts

At 200 edges, exhaustive finds enough pattern instances for a
final score of 20 237; sampling (500 samples per candidate)
reaches only 1 109 — about 5.5% of the exhaustive score. The
metric's reuse-savings term `(N−1)·k` is most of the score, and
sampling under-counts N.

At 100 edges the gap is smaller (1 160 vs 3 948 — sampling hits
~29% of exhaustive). Smaller graphs give sampling a proportionally
larger chance of hitting every instance within the sample budget.

### Final score is not directly comparable across modes

The sampling-mode score still signals "this RSet has structure"
(much higher than random), just not quite the same absolute scale
as exhaustive would give. Callers wanting to compare scores
across RSets should use **one consistent mode**.

### Steps taken is similar

Both modes took 2–3 steps before saturation. The drive's
action-selection logic (greedy, best-Δ) is the same; the main
difference is the per-step cost of pattern discovery.

====================================================================
PRACTICAL GUIDANCE
====================================================================

- **≤ 100 edges**: exhaustive is fine (fast, complete).
- **200–500 edges**: exhaustive tolerable (seconds), sampling
  is faster but loses information. Pick based on whether the
  caller needs to know every instance.
- **> 500 edges**: sampling is the only option that finishes in
  reasonable time. Accept that pattern counts are
  under-estimates; design metrics that don't require exact `N`.

### Raising sample_count

`sample_count = 500` is arbitrary. Doubling it roughly doubles
wall time while increasing coverage. Diminishing returns — at
some point every instance has been hit and extra samples are
wasted. A future ADR could make sample_count self-adaptive based
on the rate of new-instance discovery.

====================================================================
COMMITMENT CHECK
====================================================================

- Sampling path doesn't invalidate any prior mechanism; just
  trades completeness for tractability at a specific point
  (`find_instances_of` enumeration).
- Metric: still ADR 0040's combined formula.
- No new API added by this benchmark — it exercises ADR 0043's
  existing `instance_sampling` knob.

====================================================================
WHAT'S ENABLED
====================================================================

With sampling mode, v2's drive loop now runs tractably on
~1000-edge graphs — into the β+ range where real-world-sized
relational data starts to be interesting. The drive still makes
sensible decisions (score reflects structure; random input
halts), just with under-counted instance statistics.
